## What is new

- **Keys of the demo:** the program reads its keys in `keyboard_event()`. Use letters and digits: the page of the
  demo sends the arrow keys and Page Down / Page Up to the slides, even after a click in the demo, so that the
  presentation keeps them. Avoid the space bar, which some slide tools also use.
- **The codes of the keys:** on the web, compare the GLFW codes (`key == GLFW_KEY_Z`): the names of the keys
  (`glfwGetKeyName`) are not known in a browser.
- **A key and a button for the same action:** the keys call `slide::set("undo", "")`, as the buttons of the slide
  do; after a change made by the program, `slide::notify()` lets the panel and the slide show the new values.
- **Wide strokes:** WebGL draws lines one pixel wide. Each stroke is a ribbon, a mesh rebuilt while the mouse
  moves, as wide as wanted.
- **An image for the PDF:** `?sample=1` starts with a spiral already drawn; the poster of the demo is taken with it.

